The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type | test, mask | test);
if (!alg && test)
    alg = __crypto_alg_lookup(name, type, mask) ?
          ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD) : NULL;
```
Reason:
The first statement attempts to look up the algorithm with a modified type and mask, which may not account for the state of the algorithm. The second statement incorrectly returns an ELIBBAD error for algorithms that are still in the "larval" state (being initialized), treating them as if they had failed their tests, which leads to erroneous behavior when multiple processes access encrypted files.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (alg && !crypto_is_larval(alg)) {
    /* Test failed */
    crypto_mod_put(alg);
    alg = ERR_PTR(-ELIBBAD);
}
```
Reason:
This code checks if the algorithm is not in the "larval" state before returning the ELIBBAD error. By ensuring that only non-larval algorithms can trigger the error, it prevents the incorrect handling of algorithms that are still being initialized, thus fixing the bug.

